"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was significantly elevated at 86. The surgical findings included a pathological stage pT4b, positive surgical margins, invasion of the seminal vesicles, and the presence of lymph node metastasis. The biopsy revealed a higher grade (Gleason 4+4) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3). The presence of lymph node metastasis is a significant negative prognostic factor.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node metastasis in the biopsy, although the surgical report documented nodal metastasis. The positive surgical margins and extraprostatic extension are adverse. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of recurrence. The current horizon suggests a potential event within the estimated range. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent trajectory is crucial for refining this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 29.84 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 23.87–35.81 months; its coverage has not been validated."
